    "evidence_text": "Alaeddin Pasha (vizier)\n\n He was a fakih (expert in Islamic law).\n\n He continued during Orhan's Bey's reign.\n\nIshtup-Ishar\n\n The office of the vizier was the most important one at the royal court.\n\n The exact dating of Mehu is disputed in Egyptology.\n\n Other argue that he dates slightly later under king Pepy I.\n\n The parents are unknown.\n\n Mehu was bearing a high number of important titles.\n\n Several sons are mentioned in the tomb.\n\n Another son was Hetepka.\n\nIbrium\n\nIbbi-Sipish\n\n He was the son of his predecessor, Ibrium, who had been Ishar-Damu's vizier for 15 years.\n\n The king's name is translated as \"Arise, O Damu\".\n\n Kun-Damu is attested in the archives of Ebla dated two generations after his reign.\n\n According to Alfonso Archi, he was a contemporary of Sa\u02bfumu of Mari.\n\nRamose (TT55)\n\n Here appears also the vizier Amenhotep-Huy.\n\n Both viziers are also shown side by side in the temple of Soleb.\n\n It is not entirely clear whether Ramose was the southern or northern one.",
